BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — USDA’s own numbers show the treadmill (
 research hook)
DTSTART:20260831T150000Z
DTEND:20260831T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260830T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-usdacolonynumbers-20260831@savethehives.or
 g
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot. Carried over
  unchanged from the Aug 23 2026 run.\n\nExpert-authorship check: N/A. Sour
 ce is a USDA National Agricultural Statistics Service report plus trade-pr
 ess coverage (RFD-TV)\, not a peer-reviewed paper with named academic co-a
 uthors — no individual researcher to cross-check against KEY_PEOPLE_CONT
 ACTS.md.\n\nPost text:\nUSDA's own mid-2026 numbers show the treadmill in 
 action. Commercial beekeeping operations started the year with 2.81 millio
 n colonies — up 6% from a year before — but lost 16% of them in the fi
 rst quarter and another 11% in the second. Varroa mites affected more than
  a quarter of colonies by spring. The only reason the total keeps climbing
  is that beekeepers keep rebuilding: requeening\, adding packages\, replac
 ing losses as fast as they happen.\n\nThat's not colonies surviving — it
 's colonies being propped up. The non-managed colonies we map are the rare
  exception: no requeening\, no replacement\, no chemical support\, and sti
 ll here year after year. Every one we find and confirm is a real data poin
 t on how that's possible. SaveTheHives.org\n\nImage: images/facebook/honey
 bee-on-comb_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also available as _fb-portrait-1080x1
 350.jpg)\n\nAlt text: "Close-up of a honey bee on honeycomb."\n\nLink: kee
 p the trailing "SaveTheHives.org" as plain text — a photo is attached so
  no preview card will generate.\n\nSource: USDA NASS\, "Honey Bee Colonies
 " report\, released Jul 2026 (data through mid-2026): https://release.nass
 .usda.gov/reports/hcny0726.pdf — covered by RFD-TV\, Aug 11 2026: https:
 //www.rfdtv.com/honey-bee-colonies-rise-despite-persistent-health-pressure
 \n\nSuggested timing: Mon Aug 31\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nFull copy in FACEBOOK_ST
 ARTER_PACK.md — generated Aug 23 2026 run\, unchanged this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Reading the waggle dance (practical/Learn
  tab)
DTSTART:20260902T150000Z
DTEND:20260902T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260830T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-waggledance-20260902@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot. Carr
 ied over unchanged from the Aug 23 2026 run.\n\nPost text:\nEver wonder ho
 w bees tell each other where the good flowers are? It's called the waggle 
 dance — a returning forager runs a figure-eight pattern on the comb\, an
 d the angle and duration of her waggle run point the rest of the colony st
 raight to a food source\, sometimes over a mile away.\n\nIt's one of the w
 ildest examples of animal communication anywhere\, and you can learn to re
 ad the basics yourself in our Learn tab — same tab that covers beelining
 . savethehives.org/?tab=learn 🐝\n\nImage: images/facebook/learn-waggle-
 decoder_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also available as _fb-portrait-1080x1350.
 jpg)\n\nAlt text: "Illustration of a honey bee performing a waggle dance f
 igure-eight pattern on honeycomb\, showing how bees communicate flower dir
 ections and distance."\n\nLink: use savethehives.org/?tab=learn as trailin
 g plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\n
 Suggested timing: Wed Sep 2\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nFull copy — generated Aug 2
 3 2026 run\, unchanged this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — A 20-year colony in a barn wall (found-co
 lony spotlight)
DTSTART:20260906T150000Z
DTEND:20260906T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260830T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-barnwallcolony-20260906@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot. Carried
  over unchanged from the Aug 23 2026 run.\n\nFLAG FOR RONNIE — before po
 sting: built around Clay Heilman's real find (see KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md /
  Legacy_Submitter_Outreach_Tracker.xlsx) — a ~20-year wild colony in a b
 arn wall near the Snoqualmie River\, WA\, confirmed by a neighbor. He hasn
 't been asked whether it's OK to share his story or general location publi
 cly on Facebook — get his OK first\, or use the anonymized alternate bel
 ow.\n\nPost text (names Clay + general area — needs his OK first):\nOne 
 of our legacy dataset submitters just gave us the best kind of update. Cla
 y\, near the Snoqualmie River in Washington\, wrote in about a wild honey 
 bee colony that's been living in a barn wall for roughly 20 years — with
  a neighbor who can confirm the timeline. Since reconnecting\, he's alread
 y logged several new hives and checked in on ones already on the map\, and
  started scouting the river for more.\n\nThat's a Genetic Goldmine if ther
 e ever was one: two decades of surviving completely on its own. This is ex
 actly the kind of find SaveTheHives exists to capture. If you know of a hi
 ve that's been around for years\, log it — you never know how long its s
 tory already is. SaveTheHives.org\n\nAlternate post text (anonymized\, saf
 e to post without asking):\nA supporter who's been helping check on legacy
  hive records just shared one of the best finds so far: a wild honey bee c
 olony that's been living in a barn wall for roughly 20 years\, confirmed b
 y a neighbor who's watched it that whole time. Since then\, several nearby
  hives have gotten logged too — turns out one good tip often leads to mo
 re.\n\nThat's a Genetic Goldmine if there ever was one: two decades of sur
 viving completely on its own. If you know of a hive that's been around for
  years\, log it — you never know how long its story already is. SaveTheH
 ives.org\n\nImage: no dedicated asset — flagged for Ronnie\, consider as
 king Clay for a real photo of the barn (with permission). Fallback: images
 /facebook/logo_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg.\n\nAlt text: (set based on whichev
 er image is used\; if logo fallback: "SaveTheHives logo.")\n\nLink: keep t
 he trailing URL as plain text if a photo is attached — no preview card w
 ill generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Sun Sep 6\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nSource: KEY_
 PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md (Clay Heilman entry\, updates through 2026-08-22) and L
 egacy_Submitter_Outreach_Tracker.xlsx.\n\nFull copy — generated Aug 23 2
 026 run\, unchanged this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Darwin’s solution: 17 years of natural 
 selection on Oahu (research hook)
DTSTART:20260907T150000Z
DTEND:20260907T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260830T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-darwinssolution-20260907@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot — fresh res
 earch-hook search this run (Aug 30 2026)\, per FACEBOOK_POST_LOG.md's "Nex
 t up" note to resume searching starting Sep 7.\n\nExpert-authorship check:
  PARTIAL MATCH. "Darwin's solution: honeybees survive mite vectors and vir
 uses through natural selection" (Neumann\, Beaurepaire\, Bouga\, Brodschne
 ider\, Carreck\, Dahle\, de Miranda\, de Sousa\, Dietemann\, Eliash\, Elli
 s\, Erler\, Evans\, Locke\, Martin\, Mondet\, Morawetz\, Moro\, Özkırım
 \, Panziera\, Traynor and others — a ~20-author COLOSS-network consortiu
 m piece) lists James D. Ellis as a co-author — almost certainly the same
  "Jamie Ellis (U Florida)" already in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md's Batch 1 uni
 versity outreach list (not yet contacted). Worth a soft mention if outreac
 h to him ever happens\, though he's one of ~20 co-authors on an opinion pi
 ece\, not the lead — don't overstate his role. No new name worth adding 
 to KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md\; the rest of the author list is European/intern
 ational COLOSS-network researchers outside that file's US-only scope.\n\nP
 ost text:\nNew research just backed up exactly what SaveTheHives is built 
 to track. A 17-year study of a free-living honey bee population on Oahu fo
 und that colonies left alone with Varroa mites — no treatment\, no rescu
 e — crashed hard at first\, then rebounded\, evolving better mite-detect
 ion and removal behavior along the way. Natural selection did in under two
  decades what chemical treatments still can't fully solve.\n\nThe paper (T
 rends in Parasitology\, out this month) has nearly 20 co-authors from the 
 global bee-research community — including Jamie Ellis at the University 
 of Florida\, already on our own list of researchers to reach out to. Its t
 akeaway: the field needs standardized\, ongoing monitoring of colony survi
 val to find more populations like this one. That's exactly what a map of n
 on-managed colonies\, checked in on year after year\, gives us. SaveTheHiv
 es.org\n\nImage: no asset fits the Oahu-specific angle well — flagged fo
 r Ronnie. Fallback: images/facebook/oak-tree-colony_fb-square-1080x1080.jp
 g (also _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)\, last used Aug 26 — general wild-co
 lony-surviving-on-its-own imagery\, reasonable reuse at a ~12-day gap.\n\n
 Alt text: "A honey bee colony's entrance in a cavity of an old oak tree." 
 (if oak-tree fallback used)\n\nLink: keep the trailing "SaveTheHives.org" 
 as plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n
 \nSource: Neumann\, P. et al. (2026). Darwin's solution: honeybees survive
  mite vectors and viruses through natural selection. Trends in Parasitolog
 y 42(8):3-5. https://www.cell.com/trends/parasitology/fulltext/S1471-4922(
 26)00156-X\n\nSuggested timing: Mon Sep 7\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new conte
 nt this run — resumed fresh Monday research-hook search per the Aug 23 r
 un's "Next up" note. This piece is unusually on-thesis: it's an explicit c
 all for standardized\, ongoing monitoring of non-managed colony survival 
 — the exact mechanism SaveTheHives already runs as a citizen-science map
 .
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — The best time of day to go beelining (pra
 ctical)
DTSTART:20260909T150000Z
DTEND:20260909T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260830T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-besttimeofday-20260909@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot — n
 ew content this run (Aug 30 2026)\, no ready post existed for this slot.\n
 \nPost text:\nTiming matters almost as much as technique. The best window 
 for beelining is mid-morning to early afternoon on a warm\, calm\, sunny d
 ay — bees are out foraging heavily\, wind isn't scattering their flight 
 lines\, and you've got enough daylight left to follow more than one bee's 
 route. Overcast\, windy\, or late-in-the-day trips are still possible\, ju
 st harder.\n\nEarly fall (right now) is one of the best stretches of the w
 hole year for it — plenty of blooming forage still out\, and bees packin
 g in stores before the weather turns. Full how-to in the Learn tab. saveth
 ehives.org/?tab=learn 🐝\n\nImage: images/facebook/meadow-hero_fb-square
 -1080x1080.jpg (also available as _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg) — open wil
 dflower meadow\, unused in any post so far\, fits the mid-day foraging fra
 ming.\n\nAlt text: "A honey bee foraging in a sunlit wildflower meadow."\n
 \nLink: use savethehives.org/?tab=learn as trailing plain text — a photo
  is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Wed Se
 p 9\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content this run — seasonal/practical ang
 le\, timed to early fall being a strong beelining window\; first use of th
 e meadow-hero asset in a post.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — We're part of a bigger movement (communit
 y)
DTSTART:20260913T150000Z
DTEND:20260913T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260830T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-biggermovement-20260913@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot — new 
 content this run (Aug 30 2026)\, no ready post existed for this slot. Pull
 ed from the standing idea already flagged in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §6 
 ("worth a 'we're part of a bigger movement' post").\n\nPost text:\nSaveThe
 Hives isn't the only citizen-science project keeping an eye on honey bees 
 right now — and that's a good thing. Auburn University and the Apiary In
 spectors of America run an annual national beekeeping survey tracking mana
 ged-colony health across the country. Different focus (they track managed 
 hives\, we track non-managed ones)\, same underlying idea: real data comes
  from real people paying attention\, not just from labs.\n\nThe more of th
 ese efforts there are\, the fuller the picture gets. If you've logged a hi
 ve with us\, you're part of that bigger picture too. SaveTheHives.org\n\nI
 mage: images/facebook/map-screenshot-full_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also av
 ailable as _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg) — full\, uncropped map screenshot
 \, unused in any post so far.\n\nAlt text: "Screenshot of the full SaveThe
 Hives map showing hive pin locations across the region."\n\nLink: keep the
  trailing URL as plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card wil
 l generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Sun Sep 13\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new con
 tent this run — no individual researcher to expert-authorship-check (thi
 s is a peer-project shoutout\, not a research hook)\; first use of the map
 -screenshot-full asset in a post.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
